Done — refactoring of the `org.springframework.boot.logging` package.

## What I refactored

**`core/spring-boot/src/main/java/org/springframework/boot/logging/DeferredLog.java`** — consolidated the six `isXxxEnabled()` guards.

Each of the six level-check methods repeated the identical synchronization-and-null-check guard, differing only in which `Log` method it called:

```java
@Override
public boolean isTraceEnabled() {
    synchronized (this.lines) {
        return (this.destination == null) || this.destination.isTraceEnabled();
    }
}
// ...and five more, identical except isDebugEnabled/isInfoEnabled/...
```

Now the guard lives in one place and each override is a one-line delegation:

```java
@Override
public boolean isTraceEnabled() {
    return isEnabled(Log::isTraceEnabled);
}
// ...

private boolean isEnabled(Predicate<Log> enabled) {
    synchronized (this.lines) {
        return (this.destination == null) || enabled.test(this.destination);
    }
}
```

**Behavior preservation:** `enabled.test(this.destination)` *is* `this.destination.isXxxEnabled()`, evaluated under the same `this.lines` monitor with the same null-short-circuit. The `Log::isXxxEnabled` references are non-capturing unbound method references, so the JVM caches them — no per-call allocation, which matters because level checks are called per log site. Covered by `DeferredLogTests` (a dedicated test per level). Net −5 lines, and the locking logic is now defined once instead of six times.

## Why I rejected the scan's top find

The strict scan proposed extracting three `Assert.state(... != null ...)` lines shared by two methods in `StructuredLogEncoder`. I read the file and rejected it: it's the same trivial null-check-boilerplate extraction I declined earlier — it nets ~zero lines, adds indirection, and the three `create*Formatter` methods otherwise build different types from different argument sets (the third asserts a different subset), so they don't cleanly unify. The `DeferredLog` consolidation is the genuinely worthwhile one (6 occurrences, centralizes the locking), so that's what I applied.

Standing caveat: reasoned-only (verified against `DeferredLogTests`); still needs the project's Gradle + checkstyle/`spring-javaformat` gates before merge.

